[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b168093-06ee-59d2-a609-623e2ec4e449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327451} {:answer/id #uuid "4588a6ee-dc15-5f53-86b2-b586c559c781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327542} {:answer/id #uuid "4f67b91c-0f60-548d-8a33-48f8a99a74b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3\\pi }{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327627} {:answer/id #uuid "59ccd062-fdab-5575-acd3-736c657f5624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327721} {:answer/id #uuid "ebca9d1f-42e9-5b29-8145-61126df47e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebca9d1f-42e9-5b29-8145-61126df47e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328998} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fca567e-a930-5cf5-8fe9-bab4358653b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f27dcc08-cb74-544e-b846-2c5aa548a3f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-42734" :question/problem "Let $V=C[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[\\pi ,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{t}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{4}){|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi }{4})-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =(\\pi +0)-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡t=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8536c628-8b6e-548b-8d23-4c9057678c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133093} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8b5f1b-a767-50a8-a5ae-cfa35a90b28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133094} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3f6e3c-3d68-5a18-8944-ef6e47f2e1ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133095} {:answer/id #uuid "5e12b1a3-9680-5544-aa6e-1185faebe327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133096} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd5aa75-3d14-5c5c-87ee-3953607513aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133097}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ce3e6d81-4d6c-5b0a-86e0-a494790f9358" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133092}] :question/id #uuid "e78dd22e-6535-514a-9020-7853b60f7758" :question/math-academy-id "q-75516" :question/problem "What is $(\\frac{2}{5})^{2}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e37f47-83d1-5088-9b85-0e2e24287bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342305} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac14869-5cac-504f-add3-8e9d269fda7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342328} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ae148b-fb50-5f08-a2c0-8a72efe39137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343656} {:answer/id #uuid "a2572c76-84e6-542d-8804-715f0e7d0f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343665} {:answer/id #uuid "eed4f8df-a5a6-55f1-8a77-0e82d4221bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eed4f8df-a5a6-55f1-8a77-0e82d4221bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344337} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f0f7cf8-f8e0-5481-a04a-8c128f9e6dec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a42a0c64-6afa-5778-a107-25576497d815" :question/math-academy-id "q-76186" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y-1}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(t+1)\\,j\\,$ for $t\\in [\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{6}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(t+1)\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=t+1.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y-1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{(t+1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={u}^{3/2}{|}_{9}^{25} \\\\  & ={25}^{3/2}-{9}^{3/2} \\\\  & =125-27 \\\\  & =98\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c05b7dff-5734-5d67-89b3-eb14aa625f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ = \\arctan (\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133219} {:answer/id #uuid "f7356b53-9780-59b9-a3f9-dad006e9f5cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ = π - \\arctan (\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133220} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba1212d-cf14-5b0f-a312-483e7aafd9ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ = π + \\arctan (\\frac{4}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133221} {:answer/id #uuid "539e6f28-1f9f-5c2a-84d2-8dd1d0030471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ = \\arctan (\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133222} {:answer/id #uuid "62472f40-86b4-591a-9a13-8e661a8bbeae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ = π + \\arctan (\\frac{3}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133223}] :answer-field/id #uuid "441de43b-a2f9-5c12-949d-0c3ca851194b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133218}] :question/id #uuid "78a57d66-7eed-5473-b1e2-4467914403c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-8536" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a066f09d4275f0ae3811fb0671e1ebd4493706e123b3b943e87a69ef9fcfe2c.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(3, 4)$, find the polar equation of the ray."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "338f1d6d-db3e-5039-8b5f-aae22fabbd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460519} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0aa715-cd46-53c6-8496-e8659486cc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "338f1d6d-db3e-5039-8b5f-aae22fabbd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460519} :answer-field/id #uuid "e072e774-4518-5103-b30b-43265d9155f3" :answer-field/key "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461126} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63cc9190-0ae1-5996-a7f6-181e038cbfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460521} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa45487-8b6f-5b57-a845-0357f250b036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63cc9190-0ae1-5996-a7f6-181e038cbfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460521} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bad1972-2fc5-5c34-b0b0-ea00210ef3e4" :answer-field/key "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461127} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "948e577a-3aef-54e4-b34e-06b35d762788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460523} {:answer/id #uuid "fef9594e-8ffa-5e45-92ae-7b0feb6eda72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "948e577a-3aef-54e4-b34e-06b35d762788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460523} :answer-field/id #uuid "42e7f608-b7d2-507d-802e-9a170099ff23" :answer-field/key "$S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a5307a8-8a58-5d9f-8aa3-c19ff22a65ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-98524" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =0+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21bae757-f490-594c-be1d-7a2d2c77f021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360982} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6c9f0e-8b69-578c-b5ae-a56b0b59e2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361041} {:answer/id #uuid "356d8fc5-06cd-5609-b39b-96eddcad3b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361072} {:answer/id #uuid "be29d524-2692-5e36-bb76-b3ac0de7b124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361688} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0cfc9c-4409-56c9-956d-a96016bee45d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0cfc9c-4409-56c9-956d-a96016bee45d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361880} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8db0f63-bf1b-537c-b12e-a90d68712f38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff7ad628-63c0-5640-b2d2-82a36608b04e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98995" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =x{y}^{2}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f63d9a1-0137-5a36-bb60-9969b4573aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360969} {:answer/id #uuid "3699a6b4-d4d9-5e31-b162-16c5007ee57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361083} {:answer/id #uuid "89c4b85e-187b-55d0-84b5-4705fe0ffb20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361449} {:answer/id #uuid "be1533c1-05ff-53d2-b2f2-b8184c361484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361686} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0d25c8-b49e-5a74-a54a-3eb3215a8c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3699a6b4-d4d9-5e31-b162-16c5007ee57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361083} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f54e0bb-f312-5428-9eb3-1323f1fab514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f3deee5-4f24-50ef-afc8-99c0e1eb0804" :question/math-academy-id "q-99008" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-1))-0 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138069 :question/id #uuid "e85da1d1-1cf3-547c-86ce-42329475fbdd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12835" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{3}k +1 + \\dfrac{4}{3}k - 3.$" :question/worked-solution "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}k+1+\\frac{4}{3}k-3 &= (\\frac{1}{3}k+\\frac{4}{3}k)+(1-3) \\\\ &= (\\frac{1+4}{3})k+(-2) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{3}k-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138070 :question/id #uuid "30cf0891-33d3-566c-8286-4c161f84c1dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12836" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{2}(p-4q).$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the distributive law and simplifying, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(p-4q) &= (\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot p-(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 4q \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}p-\\frac{4q}{2} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}p-2q\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138071 :question/id #uuid "53c1f7ca-bcaa-59e4-9929-0695b67621cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-12837" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $-\\dfrac{1}{2}u  + \\dfrac{7}{8}u.$" :question/worked-solution "We combine the like terms by adding the coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}u+\\frac{7}{8}u &= (-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{7}{8})u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to add the coefficients, we have to convert them to have the same denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{7}{8})u &= (-\\frac{4}{8}+\\frac{7}{8})u \\\\ &= (\\frac{-4+7}{8})u \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{8}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{3}{8}u.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138072 :question/id #uuid "957b3102-6eb7-52d3-9299-54ed9bd4718e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12892" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{3}(3a+6b)+2a.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the distributive law.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(3a+6b)+2a &= (\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 3a+(\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 6b+2a \\\\ &= \\frac{3a}{3}+\\frac{6b}{3}+2a \\\\ &= a+2b+2a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+2b+2a &= a+2a+2b \\\\ &= 3a+2b\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138161 :question/id #uuid "ea238f6b-7ee7-5cb1-b124-0f07480d5980" :question/math-academy-id "e-14070" :question/problem "What is $3.4 \\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ measured in $\\textrm{mm}^2$?" :question/worked-solution "There are $10\\,\\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{cm}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{10mm}{cm})^{2} &= \\frac{10^{2}mm^{2}}{cm^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100mm^{2}}{cm^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $3.4 \\, \\textrm{cm}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 3.4 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 3.4 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2}}{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 3.4 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 3.4 \\cdot  100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 340 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3.4\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ is equivalent to $340\\,\\textrm{mm}^2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138162 :question/id #uuid "e9ce69f1-ee2f-5f1f-b3fe-e165ddc979df" :question/math-academy-id "e-14071" :question/problem "What is $0.29\\,\\textrm{m}^2$ measured in $\\textrm{cm}^2$?" :question/worked-solution "There are $100\\,\\textrm{cm}$ per $\\textrm{m}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{100\\,\\textrm{cm}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{100cm}{m})^{2} &= \\frac{100^{2}cm^{2}}{m^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10000cm^{2}}{m^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $0.29\\,\\textrm{m}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 0.29 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{m}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 0.29 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{m}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{10 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2}}{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.29 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{m}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{10 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.29 \\cdot  10 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\hspace{0.167em} 900 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} .\\end{array}}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138163 :question/id #uuid "e99aff48-d63a-559f-b97b-f565fa8894cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-14072" :question/problem "What is $0.053\\,\\textrm{km}^2$ measured in $\\textrm{m}^2$?" :question/worked-solution "There are $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ per $\\textrm{km}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,000\\,\\textrm m}{\\textrm{km}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1000m}{km})^{2} &= \\frac{1000^{2}m^{2}}{km^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1000000m^{2}}{km^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $0.053\\,\\textrm{km}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 0.053 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{km}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 0.053 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{km}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.053 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.053 \\cdot  1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 53 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{m}}^{2} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.053\\,\\textrm{km}^2$ is equivalent to $53\\,000 \\,\\textrm{m}^2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138170 :question/id #uuid "a2d2a2a1-da7b-5106-8347-3a4f71ddd619" :question/math-academy-id "e-14090" :question/problem "If $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and $y=15$ when $x=\\dfrac{3}{5}$, find the value of $y$ when $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=15$ when $x=\\dfrac{3}{5}.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =\\frac{k}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\ 15\\cdot (\\frac{3}{5}) & =k \\\\ \\frac{45}{5} & =k \\\\ 9 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{9}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=3$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{9}{3} = 3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138311 :question/id #uuid "ee3fa65e-1146-598a-a89d-33d287e88066" :question/math-academy-id "e-897" :question/problem "The frequency of a wave is given by the formula\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{v}{l},\n$$\n\nwhere $v$ represents the velocity of the wave in $\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}},$ while $l$ represents the length of the wave in meters. Find an appropriate measurement unit for frequency." :question/worked-solution "We can find the unit of frequency by substituting the given units of velocity and length into the formula and simplifying. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f & =\\frac{v}{l} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}})}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{m}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{m}\\cdot \\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{s}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of frequency is $\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{s}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138779 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c82486-3253-577c-bede-0a02c498f9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{3}-b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458635} {:answer/id #uuid "3568da3f-8e6a-5e66-a04c-51f138005543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a-\\frac{3b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458733} {:answer/id #uuid "46360383-ff00-536a-83ae-8d05da42d7bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a-b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458755} {:answer/id #uuid "5f117f77-e934-5833-bbf8-d08521e2502b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{3}-\\frac{3b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458789} {:answer/id #uuid "eee94a81-f3dc-5550-b98b-500945c6fd9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a+\\frac{3b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3568da3f-8e6a-5e66-a04c-51f138005543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a-\\frac{3b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458733} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9cf5602-d832-5032-92f6-e49d0c641515" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ddb24f3-09aa-59e7-9969-7879cb03c6cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-43919" :question/problem "$3(\\frac{a}{3}-\\frac{b}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the distributive law and simplifying, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(\\frac{a}{3}-\\frac{b}{2}) &= 3\\cdot \\frac{a}{3}+3\\cdot (-\\frac{b}{2}) \\\\ &= \\frac{3a}{3}+(-\\frac{3b}{2}) \\\\ &= a-\\frac{3b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "854454f1-4bcc-52c6-ad42-878f67084815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s^{2}m}{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135707} {:answer/id #uuid "daf416fe-995a-5181-8262-892b8380f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{kgm}{s^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135708} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb7ae7c-7954-56d8-94a6-58b87a886aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{kgs^{2}}{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135709} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ea72e5-fcdd-5131-8c88-72081844b670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s^{2}}{kgm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135710} {:answer/id #uuid "782d6854-43db-5190-887a-ad11ca28b340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{kg}{ms^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135711}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a379d69e-535a-59ae-861b-f9f09fee96b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135706}] :question/id #uuid "650abb84-8f08-5a8c-b07e-80de7af43217" :question/math-academy-id "q-45920" :question/problem "The pressure $P$ applied to a surface is related to the magnitude of the normal force $F$ and the area $A$ of the surface on contact by the equation $P = \\frac{F}{A}$. If $F$ is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{kgm}{s^{2}})$, and $A$ is measured in square meters, determine an appropriate measurement unit for pressure."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147171 :question/id #uuid "a4cc2406-5f53-5a80-9e85-9cafd1fa2576" :question/math-academy-id "e-14182" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2$." :question/worked-solution "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2$ with multiplication signs:\n$(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}|= \\underbrace{\\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}}_{{{\\color{blue}{2}}}\\text{ times}}$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}|= \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}; = \\frac{4 \\cdot 4}{5 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{16}{25}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2= \\dfrac{16}{25}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147172 :question/id #uuid "24da41ad-50c6-5654-8ebe-4c47c51ebd5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14184" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3 =$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3$ with multiplication signs:\n$(\\frac{3}{5})^{3}|= \\underbrace{\\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}}_{{{\\color{blue}{3}}}\\text{ times}}$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{3}{5})^{3}|= \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}; = \\frac{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}{5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{9 \\cdot 3}{25 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{27}{125}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\dfrac{27}{125}}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147187 :question/id #uuid "902a3945-f3e7-588c-83e3-9d74fcaacc62" :question/math-academy-id "e-15037" :question/problem "Calculate the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4$." :question/worked-solution "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4$ with multiplication signs:\n$\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^{\\color{blue}{4}} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8}}_{\\large { { {\\color{blue}4}} \\text{ times}}}$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{3}{8})^{4}|= \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8}; = \\frac{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}{8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8}; = (\\overbrace{3 \\cdot 3}^{9} \\cdot \\overbrace{3 \\cdot 3}^{9})/(\\underbrace{8 \\cdot 8}_{64} \\cdot \\underbrace{8 \\cdot 8}_{64}); = \\frac{9 \\cdot 9}{64 \\cdot 64}; = \\frac{81}{4096}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{81}{4\\,096}}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147229 :question/id #uuid "7aa4b1ba-4362-5a4b-88d4-25d9ebe2cef7" :question/math-academy-id "e-2178" :question/problem "The third term of a geometric sequence is $80$ and its fifth term is $20.$  Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive." :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_5 = 20$ and $a_3 = 80.$ Substituting $n=5$ and $m=3$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{20}{80} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147231 :question/id #uuid "3ee74da8-3a3b-5119-b34f-aee3f5770302" :question/math-academy-id "e-21832" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\sinh(3t).$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\sinh(3t) = \\dfrac{e^{3t} - e^{-3t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}}{2}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{3t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-3t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{e^{kt}\\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-k}, \\qquad s > k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\sinh(3t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{3t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{1}{s+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+3)-(s-3)}{(s-3)(s+3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\mathcal L\\{e^{3t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (3,\\infty)$ and $\\mathcal L \\{e^{-3t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty),$ we have that $\\mathcal L\\{\\sinh(3t)\\}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(3,\\infty) \\cap (-3,\\infty) = (3,\\infty),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s > 3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147243 :question/id #uuid "9a070d56-1aac-5f5d-885c-790d75571c63" :question/math-academy-id "e-2299" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^ng(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n g(x,y)$ for some $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{1}{tx}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{tx}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^{-1} g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147260 :question/id #uuid "81efbdf3-8a19-5b11-b3c3-c25abf854d50" :question/math-academy-id "e-2417" :question/problem "Which symbols could replace the empty box below to make the statement true?\n$\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 \\,\\,\\square\\,\\, \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^1$\n\n1. $>$\n2. $<$\n3. $=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{3})^{2} &= \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}}_{{{\\color{blue}2} }\\text{ times}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, for\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{4})^{1} = \\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nBecause\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} < \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nthe correct statement must be\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 < \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^1 \\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147344 :question/id #uuid "cb472181-da8e-5252-9c80-4d336e2ccf9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6615" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = 6\\cos x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\cos(3x)$ and $y_2 = \\sin(3x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x) y_1 + u_2(x) y_2\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle u_2(x) = \\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 6\\cos x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}^{′} &=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) &=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2.$ We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147357 :question/id #uuid "d058e1e9-5881-568b-bb28-bab5d6299f53" :question/math-academy-id "e-6649" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f''(t) \\right\\}$ for $s > 0,$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous on $[0,\\infty),$ $f'(0)=2,$ $f(0)=-1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} =   \\dfrac{2 - s}{s^2+4}, \\quad s>0.\n$$\n\nAssume that $f'$ is continuous and $f''$ piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $F(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty).$ If $f'(t)$ is continuous and $f''(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}L{f(t)}-sf(0)-{f}^{′}(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of $f''(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}L{f(t)}-sf(0)-{f}^{′}(0) \\\\  & ={s}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2-s}{{s}^{2}+4}-s\\cdot (-1)-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}(2-s)}{{s}^{2}+4}+s-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}(s-2)}{{s}^{2}+4}+(s-2) \\\\  & =(s-2)(-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{{s}^{2}+4}+1) \\\\  & =(s-2)(\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s-2)}{{s}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147365 :question/id #uuid "cfc54894-5add-5272-bf47-6495f5f4d288" :question/math-academy-id "e-6666" :question/problem "Find $y(t)$ if the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' +  6y = 2\\sin {2t}, \\quad y(0) = 0, \\quad y'(0) = 2\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ns^2Y(s) - 2 +6 Y(s)  =  2\\left(\\dfrac{2}{s^2 + 4}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)-2+6Y(s) & =2(\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}), \\\\ ({s}^{2}+6)Y(s) & =2+\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+6)Y(s) & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}+12}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+6)Y(s) & =\\frac{2({s}^{2}+6)}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{Y(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y(t) =  \\sin {2t}$ is the solution of the original differential equation."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd114fc0-127b-5ead-b00d-fe9bffb132d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (\\frac{2 + x^{3}}{C + x^{2}})^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144120} {:answer/id #uuid "f165fd86-7b5d-5972-b661-153f3789a251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{C - 2x^{2}}{3x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144121} {:answer/id #uuid "0435f5b5-1868-5ec4-ab51-b968a42aecfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{C + 3x^{2}}{x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144122} {:answer/id #uuid "1756d73e-167c-5559-8f61-0cad7f13c45d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{2x^{3}}{C - 2x^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144123} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8e9606-7e84-5b69-bc8a-f9aeef6bf9b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x^{3}}{C - x^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144124}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e47c4c86-e1e5-5928-8e04-a5acbcf9617c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "caf692da-64e7-571b-8ecf-524b4723203b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106074" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n$x^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{4} - xy$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9c9dada-db05-559b-9743-21f52c929fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{128}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144156} {:answer/id #uuid "cccb185f-8a2d-573c-9c3e-1efdd3646884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144157} {:answer/id #uuid "039a717f-2780-54dd-bf54-32c9b90fe1c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144158} {:answer/id #uuid "578fffb3-91e5-53ab-8942-0f319a28fe58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{512}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144159} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3b02ce-4af3-5a76-b29c-203c283a429b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{512}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144160}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c6e00de4-72ee-50bb-bf09-37d658e93235" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144155}] :question/id #uuid "7ff9f184-412f-5ecb-8655-56f2935f5b78" :question/math-academy-id "q-137662" :question/problem "What is $(\\frac{3}{4})^{4}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "25080eb9-08d3-53cb-9816-6e502b06eb61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144187}] :question/id #uuid "ba20b3d6-c6c1-5f9a-a6aa-4948fca7702e" :question/math-academy-id "q-172439" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, $(\\frac{2}{5})^{2} =$ {{field-1}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "4d132f27-534c-5ddb-be92-4a469e52c971" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144196}] :question/id #uuid "c58ffb9d-2369-52ee-b76d-dcbd2b6e9e5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-224020" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, $(\\frac{1}{3})^{3} =$ {{field-1}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147779 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d346709-8a3e-5716-980c-4391e89f2ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312070} {:answer/id #uuid "49a62fb1-d0f6-5a38-914b-9b43bf3bd451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312570} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd1edf7-25b6-5bb4-87c9-f7ff3ad3e144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312623} {:answer/id #uuid "68c456bd-0a36-5755-8ea9-91f4b02c409f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312795} {:answer/id #uuid "b405d52d-83ac-5a21-b4c8-266b60941ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b405d52d-83ac-5a21-b4c8-266b60941ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313374} :answer-field/id #uuid "562b93d8-a47c-577d-a0d7-a7f4bcff4ced" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74ee02ad-e149-5788-b38e-30cd9d9185e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312906} {:answer/id #uuid "920897cd-8c58-5e00-b94e-d3af946be6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313146} {:answer/id #uuid "922c133c-fa23-54d6-948e-55ae0cbb49a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313148} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd56e1a-f8a1-5390-a97e-f54d798b8a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313577} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b854b5-9f9d-5f56-b656-2cf7ba07bdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313660} {:answer/id #uuid "f6306507-e019-51de-a8ed-c7f3e6ef6e38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6306507-e019-51de-a8ed-c7f3e6ef6e38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313944} :answer-field/id #uuid "e32cca82-a51d-5c4b-8dd0-a686092ad64e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a4749b4-7bc7-5f25-b6f4-236f9d3ed717" :question/math-academy-id "q-340665" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that the sine function can be defined in terms of complex exponentials as follows: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=\\frac{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}-{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}-{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}-\\frac{{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}}=\\frac{1}{s-\\text{i}\\omega },\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3\\text{i}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)} & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{3\\text{i}t}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{-3\\text{i}t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-3\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}(\\frac{1}{s-3\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}(\\frac{(s+3\\text{i})-(s-3\\text{i})}{(s-3\\text{i})(s+3\\text{i})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}(\\frac{6\\text{i}}{{s}^{2}+9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bda684b-d24c-51f4-949d-8abf795268cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bda684b-d24c-51f4-949d-8abf795268cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318036} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a43a09-9bb3-5b7a-ae78-6f83251eed68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccd487bf-6852-5287-bce6-d2a3cbfbcf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345071" :question/problem "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)-y(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)-1-Y(s)=-2(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)-1-Y(s) & =-2(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}) \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =1-\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>1.$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}(Y(s)) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822041c1-cd9b-55d7-bfff-3ebb57a029d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(120)^{t} = 61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144645} {:answer/id #uuid "a7db36be-ba3a-5bf3-a816-1bfa170cd1bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120 \\cdot (\\frac{4}{5})^{t} = 61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144646} {:answer/id #uuid "c4851a92-3196-56c5-bdc7-f7f53c6b7b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{5})^{t} = 120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144647} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcf8073-0443-5e16-8c9b-22d569383727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61 \\cdot (\\frac{4}{5})^{t} = 120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144648} {:answer/id #uuid "f0fdf07d-a9c4-5de8-baaf-42a981ecf722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5} \\cdot (120)^{t} = 61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144649}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f8dc4592-2e01-5fec-ba63-31